1 OCCU Häradshövding, ståthållare, landshövding
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =Håkan Knutsson (Hand)=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Son of Knut Håkansson (Hand) to Bjerkenäs and Vret in Odensjö parish, Rangelsbo and Själsarp in Unnaryds parish (all in Jönköping County), Ekenäs in Målilla parish, Kalmar county, and Märta Arvidsdotter (Västbo-Drake).  Was it f
2 CONC irst of the family who after the gun called himself Hand. Ståthholder at Kronoberg's castle with Allbo with fl. 1588-09-05 in Reval signed the council with prayer letter, which was then handed over to King Sigismund in the name of
2 CONC  the council, nobility and war commander13. Was 1591 chieftain in Södra Vedbo here13. Signed Uppsala meeting's decision 1593. Death 1633 at Vret and buried in his own burial place in Odensjö old church, where an epitaph over him a
2 CONC nd his wife existed even in the middle of the 1760s. Inheritance was held after him 1634-01-10 between the then living children Carin, Sofia, Johan and Elisabeth.
2 CONT
2 CONT * His son Erik Hand erected a new church (made of wood) at Ols-hammar. In it he inserted the stained-glass windows, which are still counted as the most important ornaments of the church. These paintings, burned into the glass, are
2 CONC  both original and neat. Their task is to remind him of himself and his arms brothers in the Polish war. The names of them are reproduced in the paintings, which are made up of the weapons in question. These weapons are eight name
2 CONC d H. Wrangel, Ant. G. Reich, Jak. Schött, Erik Hand in Berkines, Schilt, Byrt, Tur-gend, Nachrey Hastfer. All of these stained glass paintings were made in Riga in the 1620s. 
2 CONT * These brothers were sons to the governor of Krono-berg County, [https://www.geni.com/photo/view?album_type=photos_of_me&id=6000000003232180360&photo_id=6000000096623458828&position=1 '''Håkan Knutsson and his wife, the  famous V
2 CONC irginia Eriksdotter, who was the daughter of Erik XIV. Arvid Hand died already in 1621.'''] He was in the 30-year war and joined Nuremberg. Around the church door on the west wall are four coat of arms, namely Håkan Knutsson Hands
2 CONC  and Virginia Eriksdotter, ie Erik and Arvid Hands parents. The mother's weapon is the Vasa weapon. The other two weapons belong to the Soop and Cedercreutz families.  The church was rebuilt in 1744 by Count Åke Soop. His and his 
2 CONC spouse's (Margareta TersmedenCedercreutz) coat of arms are installed in the church. The church was restored in 1758 by utility patron Karl von Wahrendorff. It then got its current look: a long- lived house with a straight finish, 
2 CONC high vaulted wooden ceiling, closed benches, red brick floor. It lacks cows. The pulpit is located above the altar. Only one other church, Brevens, has such a device. The altarpiece represents Christ in the herb garden. Not only t
2 CONC he sleeping disciples but also the comforting angel are behind Christ. At the back of the church is another altarpiece, a triplet.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Married=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Married in 1585 or 1586  [https://www.geni.com/photo/view/6000000003232180360?album_type=photos_of_me&photo_id=6000000168182462094 '''Virginia Eriksdotter,'''] born 1559-01-01 at Kalmar castle, dead 1633 after the man. She becam
2 CONC e 1566 by her father, King Erik XIV, The incidents of jealousy of Russian Grand Prince Ivan Ivanovich were natural daughter of King Erik XIV and Agda Persdotter. Virginia Eriksdotter received 1585-05 07 by King Johan six farms in 
2 CONC Västergötland and 1589-04-14 by Duke Carl five homes in Vadsbo, Skaraborg County. 
2 CONT
2 CONT =Children=
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Catharina''', born at Björkenäs. Live widow 1646. Buried in Levene church, Skaraborg County. Married in 1615 with the governor Johan Henriksson, adlad Rider (Reuter af Skälboö, no. 158), born 1585, dead 1644.
2 CONT * '''Knut''', to Bjerkenäs. Participated as a champion in the battle at Kirkholm in 1605.  Equestrian for a banner of lively remuneration riders, with whom he participated in the Kalmar Empire in 1611-1613, where he particularly d
2 CONC istinguished himself at Högsby and Säby bridge June – July 161213. Equestrian for the Kalmar fan by Småland's rider 1613-07-20 and received in connexion with this in surrender twelve farms in Bolmsö and Unnaryds snr. Joined the ri
2 CONC ders in the summer of 1613 to Estonia and participated in the Pontus de la Gardie's Russian campaign. Unmarried murdered at the beginning of 1614 at Rugils in Russia, where he was sent as a Swedish legacy. Buried in Reval's Cathed
2 CONC ral. 'While he was lying on Oland in 1611 against the Danes, he wrote a small book, called:' Sverdz-Bref, which is a Christian shield, with which a godly Krijgzman daily shall arm ', and which he dedicated to the crown prince Gust
2 CONC af Adolf. The book was reprinted in Stockholm in 1643 and 1671. 
2 CONT * '''Sofia''', levde 1634. Gift med överstelöjtnanten Baltsar von Neüman, natural. by Neuman, No. 259.
2 CONT * '''Arvid''', to Olshammar in Hammars parish, Örebro county with subordinates of over 50 homes, which goods he and his brother Erik 1621 received partly in the soil, partly in the donation of Gustaf II Adolf, and Burkhof in Livla
2 CONC nd13. Page with Prince Frans Albrekt of Mecklenburg. Sub-officer at Prince Mauritz 'of Nassau life guards in the Netherlands. Returned to Sweden in 1615. Lieutenant at the life guards in 1616. Was 1619 horseman at the Swedish nobl
2 CONC e fan. Supreme guard and captain of the Östgöta infantry regiment in 1620. Colonel 1621 for the then newly formed second eastern cemetery about eight companies and in the summer moved with his regiment to Livland and participated 
2 CONC in Riga's conquest. Death unmarried in late autumn in Riga by the plague. He received 1615-11-14 in settlement of his salary the interest rate of several home of Småland, which his deceased brother Knut had previously had.
2 CONT * '''Erik''', to Olshammar. Page at Duke Frans Albrekt al Mecklenburg.  Sub-officer at Prince Mauritz 'of Nassau life guards in the Netherlands. Lieutenant at Småland's people in 1615. Lieutenant at the life guards after the broth
2 CONC er Arvid 1617-09-14. Captain up there. 1618. Chieftain in Uppvidinge hordad s. 1625 on behalf of the family under no. 59. Colonel for all the footmen in Östergötland 1628-06-14. Switched to Germany in 1631-06-00 with its regiment.
2 CONC  Head of the Swedish Swedish Brigade, consisting of Östgöta, Jönköping and Skaraborg's regiment, s.Alte Veste at Nuremberg, and buried at 17/10 in St. Anna's Lutheran Church in Augsburg. He was one of Gustaf II Adolf's most excell
2 CONC ent field chiefs. Took as a brigade chief a glorious part in the battle of Breitenfeld in 1631-09-07 and the transition of Lech in 1632-04-05. At the attack on Alte Veste he became deadly apart and captured. His and some of his wa
2 CONC rriors' coats of arms, painted on glass in Riga in the 1620s, are in Olshammar's chapel. In Gustaf Adolf's memorial chapel at Lützen, which is adorned by the king's most distinguished warriors on glass painted weapons, Erik Hands 
2 CONC also exists
2 CONT * '''Johan'''. Häradshövding. 
2 CONT * '''Elisabet''', to Hössna and Gavlö. Born 1598, dead 1651-04-10 and buried s.30/11 in Hössna Church, Älvsborg County. Married to Lieutenant Colonel Per Joensson, adored Gyllensvärd, no. 240, dead 1664.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://sok.riksarkivet.se/sbl/Mobil/Artikel/12576 Hand, Kindred, Noble]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''By the Swedish Biographical Dictionary'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Hand, noble family, whose members in arms brought a hand o from the beginning of the 17th century used the family name. Its oldest known ancestor was the West Gothic nobleman Håkan Persson (d circa 1530). The pedigree information 
2 CONC about his parents would be incorrect. He became the father of Knut Håkansson (H 1). Her son Bengt Knutsson (d. 1604 or 1605) to Attorp in S Säm (Älvsb) was governor of Lysing (Ög) at least 1578-80 oi Dal (Ög) from 1582. He has bee
2 CONC n identified with the person with this name who was joined in Sevede o Aspeland's hd (Kalm) 1577-81, in Allbo o most of Kinnevald's hd (Kron) 1587 o castle bailiff at Vadstena 1588-94. In 1595, he became the headmaster of the Wido
2 CONC w Queen Gunilla. In 1600, he joined the court over the king's faithful rulers,
2 CONT
2 CONT Bengt Knutsson's brother Håkan Knutsson (d 1595 or 1596) to Ekenäs in Målilla (Kalm) was from 1585 to between 1592 and 1595 governor of Norra o Södra Vedbo (Jönk) o 1588 joined Allbo o most of Kinnevald's hd. He became married to 
2 CONC Erik XIV's illegitimate dtr Virginia father of Colonel Erik H (H 2). Her brother Knut Håkansson or Knut H (d 1614) to Bolmsö in Bolmsö (Jönk) lived in France o The Netherlands at least 1607—08 o made 1611 as a rider champion with 
2 CONC Anders Stuart a failed incident in Russia from Kajaneborg. Later he participated in the Skåne campaign 1612 o in the battles of Novgorod 1613-14. He wrote in 1611 Swärdz-Breef (tr 1643 and 1671), a small book of devotion for warri
2 CONC ors, apparently a translation of some foreign work. His brother Arvid H (d. 1621 or 1622) to Björkenäs in Odensjö (Kron) became, after staying in France, the Netherlands and Germany the governor of Kulling (Älvsb) in 1619, and Col
2 CONC onel 1621, and thus participated in the conquest of Riga. The brother Captain Johan H (d 1621 or 1622) to the Vret i S Unnaryd (Jönk), who in 1621 became governor of Stranda (Kalm) o was also at Riga, has left a diary from his par
2 CONC ticipation in Gustav II Adolf's free journey to Germany in 1620 (ed. in HH 8: 3, 1879).
2 CONT
2 CONT The family branch bearing the name H extruded on the mans side with Erik H in 1632 o on the women's side with Johan H's dtr 1681. However, with the genealogy of H's arms, a family branch, which originates from Bengt Knutsson's and
2 CONC  Håkan Knutsson's brother Arvid (d) remains alive. 1618), which after the grandfather took up the name Drake and eventually became governor of Kalmar county (see vol. 11). This branch of the family has been called Drake af Hagelsr
2 CONC um (bd 11).
2 CONT
