2 CONT
2 CONT * Sources: Post-medieval regest/notice:  Kålland district court AIa:14 (1697-1700) (under 26 January 1698), GLA, Literature and commentary For Humfrid and son Byting, see SoH 1958, p. 10, and cf. SDHK no. 30430 , which letter this
2 CONC  (partially) supplemented/corrected dating is based on.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT =The Genealogical Table of the Descendants of the Squire Tore Byting=
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The genealogical table of the descendants of the squire Tore Byting from 1579 occupies a special position among the genealogical books of the time. The person who compiled the investigation has tried to comprehensively present all
2 CONC  the descendants of the progenitor in five ranks. The family tree was last published and annotated by Jan Erik Almquist in Släkt och Hävd in 1958. Since Tore Byting's widow Holmfrid Jonsdotter lived on the Stola manor in Strö pari
2 CONC sh in Kålland in 1478, later genealogists have assumed that he himself also owned this farm, which Almquist doubts.1
2 CONT
2 CONT Almquist has not been able to clear up some of the obscure points in the family tree. What did Tore Byting's coat of arms look like? Were there perhaps several descendants that the family table omitted? How did Stola come to the f
2 CONC amily? Why did Ingrid Toresdotter (Byting) marry a man whose first name was Byting?
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Heraldry'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Raneke reports in Svenska medeltidsvapen (p.396 + Addendum) an upside down mussel shell as the Bytings coat of arms and refers to an essay by N C Hjorth in Personhistorisk Tidskrift 1979 about Hans Stake's gravestone in Kullings-S
2 CONC kövde with 16 coats of arms. But he points out that the exchange weapon there is attributed to Holmfrid Toresdotter, the progenitor's great-great-granddaughter. Now Tore Byting the elder's seal is actually preserved, although not 
2 CONC in the original but in a copy from the 16th century in Lars Sparre's copy book (16 f. 251 v. RA). It concerns a deed of gift dated Lödöse 17/7 1448, where a gunsmith Nils Baat Sigvortsen (bull's head) gives his wife Katarina Birge
2 CONC rsdotter (lilyhock weapon) in Åby, Landa parish a number of farms in the Falköping area (in Grolanda, Jäla and Marka parishes). She was the widow of Simon Gudmundsson, who gave her morning gift 24/6 1443 (Jan Liedgren in PHT 1959)
2 CONC . Among the 12 fasts, Tore Byting, Sigge Byting and Harald Byting are listed and the former was asked to seal the letter together with the later district chief of Kålland Magnus Lamb to Degeberg (squared shield).2 One gets the imp
2 CONC ression that Sigge and Harald are young boys who were allowed to accompany his father at the wedding and entrusted with holding the gallows, but still lacking his own personal seal. Perhaps they died unmarried or childless and the
2 CONC refore not included in the family register. In any case, the letter shows that Tore Byting was still alive in 1448 – nine years later than what is reported in the literature.
2 CONT
2 CONT The seal contains three objects, which resemble hockey sticks, arranged in a row (on top of each other). Perhaps a misunderstanding of the mussel shell's beads? Regarding the tinctures, a coat of arms on the Ekeblad main banner in
2 CONC  Strö church shows a white mussel shell on a blue field. Another Byting coat of arms appears on Mauritz Posse's genealogy in the Skara diocese library where a red beam in a white field represents "Holmfrid, Tord Byting's daughter"
2 CONC  (Raneke aa. Till. p. 396). A number of people with the surname Byting or Pyting appear during the 15th century in both Sweden and Finland without being able to connect them genealogically with the Kållands family.3
2 CONT
2 CONT In Norway, on the other hand, Tore Byting had descendants - which Almquist omits to report - which were treated by Henning Sollied in Norsk Slektshistorisk Tidsskrift (6. 1938 p. 160 ff). If Almquist had taken part in Sollied's in
2 CONC vestigation, the problem with the "Byting Olofsson" married into the family would have had a simple solution. Ingrid Toresdotter (Byting) was married to Olof Trulsson, a brother of Herman Trulsson, bishop in Hamar 1488–1503. It wa
2 CONC s one of their three sons Tore, Truls and Herman, who used both the family name (from the mother's side) and the patronymic and called himself Tore Byting Olofsson. Bishop Herman in Hamar had, among other things, a clam in his coa
2 CONC t of arms - perhaps also influenced by the coat of arms of his brother Olof's wife Ingrid. Or has the influence gone in the opposite direction?4
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Stola Mansion'''
2 CONT
2 CONT How then did the Stola manor come into the possession of the Byting family? Makarna Sawyer mentions in their book Before Alingsås became a city (1985 p. 69) that in the 1450s the chieftain of Älvsborg Gustav Olofsson (older Stenbo
2 CONC cksätten) seized the squire Björn Andersson's frälsegårdar in Vättle county because he refused to transfer from Karl Knutsson to Kristian I. Björn Andersson then took refuge in the county of Kålland in Skaraborg. A judgment by the
2 CONC  district court in Vättle 30/11 1527 (VFT I: 4–5 no. 53) leads further. There it is certified that Björn "Anundsson" was deprived of several farms in Lerum (trol 1454–1455, when Älvsborg was betrayed to the Danes) by the knight Gu
2 CONC staf Olofsson and then fled to "Storda på Kålland". Possibly Tore Byting's widow Holmfrid has remarried this Björn Andersson. In any case, Tore Byting's family was connected to Stola as early as 1449 and 1452. Ownership of the far
2 CONC m passed first to the daughter Ingegerd married to Per Larsson (Sparre af Rossvik), who also called himself Peder Bonde, in his first marriage and then during 1480- the number of the daughter Karin married to Klaus Ribbing (not be
2 CONC longing to the introduced family with this name). Their son Nils Klasson, with a crescent moon in his coat of arms, evidently lived at Stola until his death in 1530 and was succeeded by his daughter Agneta Nilsdotter, who had move
2 CONC d there in 1533 with her second
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT =Notes=
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 1 The letter of 25/7 1478 dated Stola has been partly misunderstood by both Almquist and Raneke. The former identifies the living mentioned son Byting with the seagull "Byting Olofsson" (which never existed), the latter interprets
2 CONC  the text as if Tore Byting's widow Holmfrid Jonsdotter (who issued the donation letter) is already dead but on the other hand correctly interprets the son Byting as still alive . For copy of the letter I thank file. dr Hans Gilli
2 CONC ngstam.
2 CONT
2 CONT An important letter, dated Stola 4/4 1452, has apparently been overlooked by research. From this it can be deduced that Holmfrid Jonsdotter was then a widow partly because she acts alone in business matters, partly because you fin
2 CONC d "God bless his soul" added after her husband Tore Byting's name. It is the husband's affairs with Mr. Åke Jönsson (Svarte Skåning, ÄSF I:3 p. 264), who had Åkerö in Södermanland as a seat farm, which is on the tape and a Jöns Br
2 CONC oka, "my close cousin", is authorized to manage them.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is possible that Holmfrid Jonsdotter entered into a new marriage around 1455 with the squire Björn Andersson from Lerum, because according to reliable information (see above) he then moved to Stola in Kålland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Raneke reports (aa. p. 255) a priest Jon Broka b. 1366–1407 with a severed human foot in the shield as belonging to Otterstad, where Holmfrid Jonsdotter apparently had his roots, and likewise (p. 353) a squire Jon Broka b. 1390 in
2 CONC  Otterstad with an eagle's foot in the shield. Perhaps Holmfrid's coat of arms with a four-legged animal(?) is in fact a misunderstood eagle's foot. Any of these could be her father. When Styffe (Scandinavia under the Union time 2
2 CONC . opp. p. 126) gives Holmfrid Jonsdotter's husband the name Jon Byting, he is apparently on the wrong track.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2 He served as district chief 1465–1481 according to J E Almquist in Lagsagor og domsagor II p. 95.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3 The personal name archive in Uppsala has information about a Claus Byting in Finland, who had a daughter Ingeborg married to Henrik Spore (Nådendal 7/3 1442). When this Ingeborg (Byting) was identified (Raneke aa. p. 396, Addend
2 CONC um) with the Ingeborg, who married in 1449 5/10 at Stola with Sven Jonsson to Dingelvik (half lily), this is obviously a mistake.
2 CONT
2 CONT 4 See family tree ibid. p. 171. Henning Sollied, who died in 1945, was a very talented and conscientious researcher who above all devoted himself to Norway's medieval salvation. We can compare him with K H Karlsson in our country,
2 CONC  who in a short time, a few years around the turn of the century, processed almost the entire older Swedish nobility.
2 CONT
2 CONT 5 Stola's ownership record during the 15th century in Swedish castles and manors (Vgl part I p. 435) only includes two names: Aron Bengtsson (Ulv) in the middle of the 15th century and Peter Larsson (Sparre) around 1480. Aron Beng
2 CONC tsson's daughter Ebba was married to the district chief and councilor Nils Klasson (Stolaätt, Raneke aa. p. 515) but there is no indication that her father would have owned land in Vgl. (ÄSF I:3 p. 304 f). On the other hand, Nils 
2 CONC Klasson had inherited Stola from his mother Karin Toresdotter (Byting), who probably took over the farm in the early 1480s, when his sister Ingegerd died and her husband Peder Bonde remarried with a daughter (whose name is unclear
2 CONC ) to Ture Jönsson Svarte Skåning (ÄSF I:3 p. 263 f) and moved to Påtorp in Vedens härad (SoH 1958 p. 11 tab. 18). According to ÄSF (I:3 p. 305), Nils Klasson who brought a white crescent in a blue field is known from 1504–1533. As
2 CONC  early as 1492, however, he is mentioned as a seal witness in a letter listed by Rasmus Ludvigsson among Per Nilsson's land deeds to Fly (B 22 p. 75 RA). That it really is Nils Klasson to Stola is evident from the seal with the cr
2 CONC escent moon, which is clearly marked in the margin. Otherwise, the identification is made difficult by the fact that another Nils Klasson (Sparre av Ellinge) is active in Western Sweden at the same time, where this Scanian family 
2 CONC had interests to guard through intermarriage with the older family Hjerne, which was possessed in Småland and Bohuslän (Norway). For the copies of Per Nilsson's land deed, I thank file. dr Hans Gillingstam and for information on T
2 CONC ore Byting's Norwegian descendants file. stomach Cary Gustafsson.
2 CONT
2 CONT Regarding the length of ownership of Stola during the 15th century, a "Sven i Stola" figures around the middle of the century. He is called the grandfather of the courtier Otte Torbjörnsson (bird) at Älvsborg, who was executed in 
2 CONC 1475 accused of privateering (Jan Liedgren: Kammarmästaren Ingemar Ragvaldsson - - Sveriges Släktforskareförbund's yearbook 1988 p. 237). Could he be identical to the Sven Jonsson in Dingelvik (half lily) who in 1449 married Ingeb
2 CONC org Toresdotter (Byting) at Stola? In that case, three seagulls to Tore Byting i.e. one after the other have written to Stola:
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1) '''Sven Jonsson (half lily) g.m. Ingeborg'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * 2) '''Per Bonde (Sparre af Rossvik) g.m. Ingegerd'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * 3) '''Claus Ribbing (crescent) g.m. Karin'''
2 CONT
2 CONT =GEDCOM Note=
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Tore Gamle Pytting, born about 1390 in Stamhuset Stola, Kållandsø, Sweden, d. before 1478 in Stamhuset Stola, Kållandsø, Sweden. He married Holmfrid at Stola.
2 CONT
2 CONT Holmfrid på Stola, b. about 1400, d. mentioned 1478 in Stamhuset Stola, Kållandsø, Sweden.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thore Pytting was born in ca. 1390 Chair. He died in Stola on Kållandsøy in Veneren, died after 1448. He married Holmfrid Jonsdatter. Thore was born in Bodde 1416 in Sweden..
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Other marriages:'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Unknown
2 CONT
2 CONT Mentioned as "gamble Thore Pytting (Byting) on Stola". In a tablet from 1579, it is stated that old Thore Pytting was married to Holmfrid. A hub on Kållandsø is still called Otterstad and in the Middle Ages the whole parish where 
2 CONC Stola is located was called Otterstadha pastorat. In Magnus Barfot's saga (1093-1103) "Kvaldens øy" is mentioned which must be the current Kållandsø. King Inge had a wooden castle built here and put 300 men there under the command
2 CONC  of Sigurd Ullstreng and Finn Skofteson. The castle on Kållandsø has a monumental location with a wide view towards Veneren and Kinnekulle. The oldest parts of the buildings date from the 13th century, when it was the permanent ca
2 CONC stle of the scarab bishops.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1615, Jacob De la Gardie sat with the castle with the surrounding parish and county. But the castle's heyday came under his son Magnus Gabriel De la Gardies, and he built the fourth building, the castle church and the knight's 
2 CONC hall. During the blasting of the plot, they used burning pieces of pork to heat up the rock, and then hit super-cold water, 300 fat pigs were supposed to have gone along with the blasting.
2 CONT
2 CONT Magnus hired professionals from Germany, the castle has a total of 248 rooms and is open to visitors. Particularly beautiful are the Hall of Knights, the Hall of Peace and Princess Euphrosyne's residence. Large parts of the archip
2 CONC elago are outside the reserve. You can take excursions with M/S Magnus Gabriel.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources: B.O.Stuve, Vår Vegvisare Sverige 1996 and In Skaraborgsbygd by Vestgøta-Bengtsson.
2 CONT
2 CONT The family tree of the weapon Tore Byting's descendants from 1579 takes a unique position among the family books of the time. The person who compiled the report has tried to comprehensively present all the descendants of the ances
2 CONC tor in five stages. The family tree was last published and annotated by Jan Erik Almquist in Släkt och Hävd 1958.
2 CONT
2 CONT Since Tore Byting's widow Holmfrid Jonsdotter lived in 1478 on the manor house Stola in Strö parish in Kålland, later genealogists have assumed that he also owned this farm himself, which Almquist doubts. 1
2 CONT Almquist has not been able to clear some of the obscure points in the family tree. What did Tore Byting's coat of arms look like? There were perhaps several descendants, somsläkttavlan utelämnat? How did Stola come to the family? 
2 CONC Why did Ingrid Toresdotter (Byting) marry a man whose surname was Byting? Heraldry Raneke reports in Svenska medeltidsvapen (p.396 + Addendum) a downturned mussel shell as ätten Byting's coat of arms and refers to an essay by N CH
2 CONC jorth in Personhistorisk Tidskrift 1979 about Hans Stake's gravestone in Kullings-Skövde with 16 coats of arms. But he points out that the coat of arms is then attributed to Holmfrid Toresdotter, the ancestor's great-great-grandda
2 CONC ughter. The seal of Tore Byting the Elder is actually preserved, although not in the original but in a copy from the 16th century in Lars Sparre's copybook (16f. 251 v. RA). It concerns a gift deed dated Lödöse 17/71448, in which 
2 CONC a gunsmith Nils Baat Sigvortsen (bull's head) gives his wife Katarina Birgersdotter (lilyhökvapen) in Åby, Landa parish a number of farms in the Falköping district (in Grolanda, Jäla and Marka parishes). She left behind Simon Gudm
2 CONC undsson, who gave her the morning gift 24/6 1443 (JanLiedgren in PHT 1959). Among the 12 fastarna uppräknas Tore Byting, SiggeByting and Harald Byting and the first-mentioned is asked to seal the letter together with the later här
2 CONC adshövdingen in Kålland Magnus Lamb till Degeberg (quadrerad sköld).2 One gets the impression that Sigge and Harald are young boys, who were allowed to accompany their father to the wedding and entrusted with the atthålla on the h
2 CONC ead, but still missing his own personal seal. Perhaps they were widowed or childless and therefore not included in the family register. In each case, the letter shows that Tore Byting was still alive in 1448 - nine years later tha
2 CONC n what is reported in the literature.
2 CONT
2 CONT The seal contains three objects, which look like hockey sticks, stacked one on top of the other. Perhaps a misconception of mussel shell tumours? Regarding the tinctures, a coat of arms on the oak leaf head banner in Strö church s
2 CONC hows a white mussel shell in a blue field. Another byting coat of arms appears on Mauritz Posse's genealogy in the Skara diocese library, where a red beam in a white field represents "Holmfrid, Tord Bytingsdotter" (Raneke aa. Add.
2 CONC  p. 396). A majority of people with the surname Byting or Pyting occur during the 15th century in both Sweden and Finland without being able to connect them genealogically with the Kållandssläkten.3
2 CONT
2 CONT In Norway, on the other hand, Tore Byting had descendants - which Almquist fails to mention - which was covered by Henning Sollied in Norsk SlektshistoriskTidsskrift (6. 1938 p. 160 ff). Had Almquist taken part in the Sollied inve
2 CONC stigation, the problem with the intermarriage "Byting O
2 CONT
2 CONT Noter 1 Brevet av den 25/7 1478 daterat Stola har delvis missförståtts både av Almquist och Raneke. Den förre identifierar den som levande om nämndesonen Byting med mågen "Byting Olofsson" (som aldrig har funnits), den senare tolk
2 CONC ar texten som om Tore Bytings änka Holmfrid Jonsdotter (som utfärdat donationsbrevet) redan är död men tolkar däremot riktigt sonen Byting som ännu levande. För kopia av brevet tackar jag fil. dr Hans Gillingstam.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ett viktigt brev, daterat Stola 4/4 1452, har tydligen förbisetts avforskningen. Av det kan man utläsa att Holmfrid Jonsdotter då var änkadels därför att hon agerar ensam i affärsangelägenheter, dels för att manfinner tillagt "Gud
2 CONC  hans själ nåde" efter maken Tore Bytings namn. Det ärmakens affärer med herr Åke Jönsson (Svarte Skåning, ÄSF I:3 s. 264),vilken hade Åkerö i Södermanland till sätesgård, som är på tapeten och enJöns Broka, "min närskylde frände"
2 CONC , får fullmakt att sköta dem.
2 CONT
2 CONT Möjligen har Holmfrid Jonsdotter ingått ett nytt gifte ca 1455 medväpnaren Björn Andersson från Lerum, eftersom denne enligt trovärdigauppgifter (se ovan) då flyttar till Stola på Kålland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Raneke redovisar (aa. s. 255) en prästman Jon Broka k. 13661407 med enavhuggen människofot i skölden som hemmahörande i Otterstad, där HolmfridJonsdotter tydligen hade sina rötter och likaså (s. 353) en väpnare JonBroka k. 1390 i
2 CONC  Otterstad med en örnfot i skölden. Kanske är Holmfridsvapen med ett fyrfota djur(?) i själva verket en missförstådd örnfot.Någon av dessa skulle kunna vara hennes far. När Styffe (Skandinavienunder unionstiden 2. upp. s. 126) ger
2 CONC  Holmfrid Jonsdotters make namnetJon Byting, är han tydligen på villovägar.
2 CONT
2 CONT Han tjänstgjorde som häradshövding 14651481 enligt J E Almquist iLagsagor och domsagor II s. 95. Personnamnsarkivet i Uppsala har uppgifter om en Claus Byting i Finland,som hade en dotter Ingeborg gift med Henrik Spore (Nådendal 
2 CONC 7/3 1442).När denna Ingeborg (Byting) identifierats (Raneke aa. s. 396, Tillägg)med den Ingeborg, som gifte sig 1449 5/10 på Stola med Sven Jonsson tillDingelvik (halv lilja) är detta uppenbarligen ett misstag.
2 CONT
2 CONT Se släkttavla ibid. s. 171. Henning Sollied, som avled 1945, var enmycket begåvad och samvetsgrann forskare som framför allt ägnade sig åtNorges medeltida frälse. Vi kan jämföra honom med K H Karlsson hos oss,vilken på kort tid nå
2 CONC gra år kring sekelskiftet bearbetade så gott somhela den äldre svenska adeln.
2 CONT
2 CONT Stolas ägarlängd under 1400-talet i Svenska slott och herresäten (Vgl delI s. 435) upptar bara två namn: Aron Bengtsson (Ulv) vid 1400-talets mittoch Peter Larsson (Sparre) omkr. 1480. Aron Bengtssons dotter Ebba vargift med härad
2 CONC shövdingen och riksrådet Nils Klasson (Stolaätt, Raneke aa.s. 515) men ingen antydan finns om att hennes far skulle ägt jordegendomi Vgl. (ÄSF I:3 s. 304 f). Däremot hade Nils Klasson ärvt Stola efter sinmor Karin Toresdotter (Byt
2 CONC ing), som antagligen tillträdde gården i börjanav 1480-talet, när systern Ingegerd avlidit och hennes make Peder Bondeingått nytt gifte med en dotter (vars namn är oklart) till Ture JönssonSvarte Skåning (ÄSF I:3 s. 263 f) och fly
2 CONC ttat till Påtorp i Vedens härad(SoH 1958 s. 11 tab. 18). Nils Klasson som förde vit halvmåne i blåttfält är enligt ÄSF (I:3 s. 305) känd 15041533. Redan 1492 finns han docknämnd som sigillvittne i ett brev förtecknat av Rasmus Lu
2 CONC dvigsson blandPer Nilssons till Fly jordebrev (B 22 s. 75 RA). Att det verkligen rörsig om Nils Klasson till Stola framgår av sigillet med halvmånen, som ärtydligt avtecknat i marginalen. Annars försvåras identifieringen av atten 
2 CONC annan Nils Klasson (Sparre av Ellinge) samtidigt är verksam iVästsverige, där denna skånska ätt fått intressen att bevaka genomingifte i den äldre ätten Hjerne, som var besutten i Småland och Bohuslän(Norge). För kopiorna av Per N
2 CONC ilssons jordebrev tackar jag fil. dr HansGillingstam och för uppgifter om Tore Bytings norska avkomlingar fil.mag. Cary Gustafsson.
2 CONT
2 CONT Beträffande ägarlängden på Stola under 1400-talet figurerar en "Sven iStola" kring århundradets mitt. Han kallas morfar till hövitsmannen OtteTorbjörnsson (fågel) på Älvsborg, vilken avrättades 1475 anklagad förkaperi (Jan Liedgre
2 CONC n: Kammarmästaren Ingemar Ragvaldsson - -
2 CONT
2 CONT SverigesSläktforskareförbunds årsbok 1988 s. 237). Kan han månne vara identiskmed den Sven Jonsson i Dingelvik (halv lilja) som 1449 gifte sig på Stola med Ingeborg Toresdotter (Byting)? I så fall skulle tre mågar till ToreByting 
2 CONC d.ä. efter varandra ha skrivit sig till Stola:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1) Sven Jonsson (halv lilja) g.m. Ingeborg 2) Per Bonde (Sparre af Rossvik) g.m. Ingegerd 3) Claus Ribbing (halvmåne) g.m. Karin
2 CONT
