1 NAME Hugh /Smyth/
2 GIVN Hugh
2 SURN Smyth
2 _MARNM Smyth
2 NICK smythe
2 NICK Smith
1 NAME Sir Hugh Smyth, of Long Ashton
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 17 NOV 1574
2 ADDR
3 CITY London
3 STAE England
1 DEAT
2 DATE 15 APR 1627
2 PLAC Long Ashton
2 ADDR
3 CITY Bristol
3 STAE England
1 TITL Sir
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Hugh Smyth and Elizabeth Gorges married in 1596
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Hugh Smyth  of Ashton Court, knighted in 1603, married Dame Elizabeth Gorges who was the eldest daughter of Sir Thomas Gorges of (then Langford) Longford Castle in Wiltshire. Sir Thomas Gorges had been a Gentleman Usher at Cou
2 CONC rt and marrried an acknowledged beauty of the day, the Swedish-born widow of the Marquess of Northampton. She had previously been one of Queen Elizabeth's Maids of Honour. The original sections of 'Langford' date from between 1584
2 CONC  and 1591.
2 CONT
2 CONT There were five daughters of the marriage between Hugh Smyth and Elizabeth Gorges; and they had one son, Thomas Smyth. Thomas Smyth's father died in 1625. His mother re-married - to her cousin, Sir Ferdinando Gorges. It is known t
2 CONC hat Sir Ferdinando Gorges and the family of Smith/Smyth/Smyth of 'Customer' Smythe were closely linked and involved in the development of the American colonies.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was said to suffer from depression and was reluctant to receive visitors at Ashton Court. There is a painting of him on display at the Red Lodge Museum, Bristol. see http://mshed.org/explore-contribute/themes/in-our-houses/ever
2 CONC yday-experiences-family-relationships/a-happy-home-at-ashton-court/sir-hugh-smyth-1575-1627/
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT It is most likely, but not yet proven, that this Hugh Smyth fathered (in 1590) the William Smyth who was brought to Ireland circa 1630.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh’s Sister Anne:
2 CONT
