1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Ferdinando Gorges==
2 CONT '''Sir Ferdinando Gorges (1565–1647), the "Father of English Colonization in North America", was an early English colonial entrepreneur and founder of the Province of Maine in 1622.  Curiously, he never set foot on American soil. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Gorges was born in Clerkenwell, Middlesex, England around 1565, descended from a cadet branch''' of the Russells of Kingston Russell, Dorset.  '''He was buried on 14 May 1647 in Long Ashton, Somerset, England. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Parents''':  Edward Gorges (1536-1567), of Wraxall, Somerset, England and Cecily Lygon, who married John Vivian after Edward Gorges' death. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Married:
2 CONT
2 CONT # on '''24 Feb 1589 in St. Margaret's Church, Westminster, England, to Ann''' (?-1620), daughter of Edward Bell, Esq. of Writtle, Essextmire.  She died 26 Aug 1620 after 31 years of marriage. 
2 CONT
2 CONT # on '''21 Dec 1621 in Exeter, England, to Mary''' (?-1622), the widow of Thomas Achims, Esq. of Pelint, Cornwall, and daughter of Thomas Fulford, Esq. of Devonshire. 
2 CONT
2 CONT # '''on 6 Dec 1627 in Ladock, Cornwall, to Dame Elizabeth''' (?-1627), daughter of Tristam Gorges, Esq. of St. Bedeaux, Devonshire; widow of William Bligh. [9]
2 CONT # on '''28 September 1629 at Wraxall, Somerset, England, to Elizabeth Gorges''' (?-1658), daughter of Sir Thomas Gorges and widow of Sir Hugh Smyth of Ashton Court. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Children of Ferdinando Gorges and Ann Bell:
2 CONT
2 CONT # John Gorges, b. 23 Apr 1593 - d. 1656, m. 1. Lady Frances Clinton alias Fiennes.  m. 2. Mary Meade.  Inherited the province of Maine from his father. 
2 CONT
2 CONT # [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_Gorges '''Robert Gorges'''] b. est 1595 in London, England, and d abt 1624 in England.  After serving in the Venetian wars, Gorges was given a commission as Governor-General of New England an
2 CONC d emigrated to modern Weymouth, Massachusetts in 1623, building his settlement on the site of the failed Wessagusset Colony.
2 CONT
2 CONT # Ellen Gorges
2 CONT
2 CONT # Honoria Gorges died young
2 CONT
2 CONT From: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ferdinando_Gorges
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Notes==
2 CONT
2 CONT * from http://www.pilgrimhall.org/patentgorges.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT >Ferdinando Gorges today is little remembered.  He founded no permanent colonies and most of his ventures were failures.  His legacy rests on his enthusiastic and visionary support for other, more successful enterprises.
2 CONT
2 CONT >Ferdinando Gorges said of himself (in modernized English)
2 CONT
2 CONT >“'''I dealt not as merchants or tradesmen are wont, seeking only to make my own profit, my ends being to make perfect the thorough discovery of the Country (wherein I waded so far with the help of those that joined with me) as I 
2 CONC opened the way for others, to make their gain, which has been the means to encourage their followers to prosecute it to their advantage'''.”
2 CONT
2 CONT >
2 CONT
2 CONT ==The Peirce Patent of 1621==
2 CONT
2 CONT from http://www.pilgrimhall.org/PatentIntro.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT >The legal arrangements under which the Pilgrims journeyed to America and established their colony, and which, ultimately, resulted in the colony’s demise in 1692 are among the most confusing aspects of the Plymouth experience.  T
2 CONC he “correct” way to proceed, as outlined in the surviving documents (and many documents do not survive), did not always reflect how affairs were actually conducted. ...
2 CONT
2 CONT >The earliest surviving state document for New England, '''the Second Peirce Patent''''''' is on display in Pilgrim Hall Museum.  It is signed by five wealthy, influential and adventurous Englishmen: '''Lennox, Hamilton, Warwick, 
2 CONC Sheffield, Gorges'''''''.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Biographical Summary==
2 CONT
2 CONT * from http://www.pilgrimhall.org/PatentGorges.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT >Ferdinando Gorges grew up a “West Country” boy, inspired by the exploits of Queen Elizabeth’s already-legendary West Country  “sea dogs” - the Gilbert brothers (John, Humphrey and Adrian), Sir Walter Raleigh, Francis Drake, John 
2 CONC Hawkins and Sir Richard Grenville.  These daring and gallant heroes of privateering exploits had made their names by striking terror into Spanish hearts and by spearheading England’s colonial expansion through exploration and colo
2 CONC nizing adventures.
2 CONT
2 CONT >All were loosely related – including young Ferdinando - and all shared a love of the sea, bold and intrepid characters, a yearning for profit and a strong sense of English identity.  .... [see pdf file, attached]
2 CONT
2 CONT * from: http://www.famousamericans.net/sirferdinandogorges/
2 CONT
2 CONT >Gorges ... was engaged in the conspiracy of Essex, and testified against the latter at his trial for treason in 1601. During the war with Spain he served in the royal navy with distinction, and in 1604 was appointed governor of P
2 CONC lymouth. 
2 CONT
2 CONT >Being a friend of Sir Walter Raleigh, he became interested in the latter's plans for colonization in the New World; and when Weymouth returned from New England in 1605, bringing five Indians, Gorges took three of them, Manida, Sk
2 CONC etwarroes, and [http://webspot1.info/calebj/squanto.html '''Tisquantum known as Squanto'''], into his home, and after instructing them in the English language gained much information relative to their country, and determined to be
2 CONC come a proprietor of land beyond the Atlantic. 
2 CONT
2 CONT >His efforts resulted in the formation of the Plymouth, which with the London company was incorporated in 1606. Between these was divided the territory extending fifty miles inland from the 34th to the 45th parallel of north latit
2 CONC ude. Plymouth company had the northern portion, which was styled North Virginia. The patentees were authorized to maintain the government for twenty-one years, with permission to impose taxes, to coin money, and to exercise all th
2 CONC e power of a well-organized society. 
2 CONT
2 CONT >After several unsuccessful expeditions, two ships were despatched from Plymouth in 1607, bearing a party who erected a fortified storehouse, near the mouth of the Kennebec, in Maine, which they called Fort George. Owing to the se
2 CONC verity of the climate and many hardships, this colony was abandoned in the following spring.   ...
2 CONT
2 CONT > In 1631 a grant of land was made to several persons, including Ferdinando Gorges, a grandson of Sir Ferdinando. This territory was situated on the Acomenticus River, and several settlements were made there ... 
2 CONT
2 CONT >In 1639 ... Gorges obtained a new charter, which constituted him lord-proprietary of the province of Maine, with extraordinary governmental powers, which were to be transmissible with the property to his heirs and assigus.  He pr
2 CONC epared to visit New England, but the company became embarrassed for funds, and was obliged to sell the ship and pinnace which had been built. The first general court of this government, which exercised the powers of an "executive,
2 CONC  legislative, and judicial body in the name of Sir Ferdinando Gorges, late proprietor of Maine," was held in Saco, 25 June, 1640. 
2 CONT
2 CONT >On the death of Sir Ferdinando [1647], the estate was left to his son, JOHN, who totally neglected the province. After writing repeatedly to the heirs and receiving no replies, the Gorges colonies formed themselves into a body po
2 CONC litic for the purpose of self-government, and submitted to the jurisdiction of Massachusetts.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==The St. Budeaux Church, Devon, England ==
2 CONT
2 CONT from http://genuki.cs.ncl.ac.uk/DEV/StBudeaux/Stabb-StBudeaux.html
2 CONT
2 CONT >ST. BUDEAUX. St. Budeaux or St. Budock. This small church [plate 215a] dating from about 1563 is situated on the top of a hill from which there are very fine views of the surrounding country. ... The principal objects of interest
2 CONC  which the church contains will be found in the mural monuments, and an altar tomb at the east end of the north aisle [plate 215b]. The latter is composed of slate and has coats of arms on the front and sides. The slab has three c
2 CONC oats of arms, and the back has a coat of arms in the centre and the following inscription:-
2 CONT
2 CONT # Roger Budockshead of Budockshead Esquire ob: 1576
2 CONT # Sir William Gorges Kn: ob: 1583
2 CONT # Dame Winifred Gorges, ob: 1599
2 CONT # Tristram Gorges of Budockshead Esq: ob: 1607
2 CONT # Mrs. Elizabeth Gorges ob: 1607
2 CONT
