0 @N6000000006127601609@ NOTE Föddes den 11 December 1634, var hovjunkare hos Karl X Gustaf och blev sedermera ryttmästare. Tillsammans med sin svåger, friherre Gustaf Adolf Skytte, avseglade han i Augusti 1661 från Kalmar, i avsikt att företa plundringar, och
1 CONC  denna färdtyckes inte ha varit deras första i denna avsikt. Vid Göteborg mötte de några holländska handelsfartyg, vilka de på Drakes förslag beslöt att överfalla. Med sin jakt följde de fartygen på avstånd in i Östersjön, och när
1 CONC  flottiljen der skildes åt,eftersatte de en av bojertarna. Omkring fem mil från Bornholm skreds till anfall. Besättningen vägrade till en början att angripa, och Skytte, som vid tillfället var drucken, visade sig missmodig; men Dr
1 CONC akes hot och uppmuntringar verkade. Fartygetäntrades, dess besättning, fem man, mördades, liken kastades i havet, fartyget fördes till ön Jungfrun i Kalmar sund och sänktes. Lasten, som bestod af vin, tobak och specerier, fördes t
1 CONC ill Skyttes gods Strömsrum i Ålems socken av Kalmar län,Sverige. Saken kunde i längden ej hemlighållas, och en rättegång inleddes. (Handlingar i detta mål förvaras i Riksarkivet.) Drake lyckades hålla sig undan, men återkom efter 
1 CONC en tid till Sverige. Han avled den 16 November 1684. - Drake är hjälten i romanen "Fribytaren på Östersjön", af Viktor Rydberg.
0 @N6000000003216083849@ NOTE ENCYCLOPÆDIA BRITANNICA
1 CONT
1 CONT Gorges, Sir Ferdinando
1 CONT
1 CONT b. c. 1566,, probably at Wraxall, Somerset, Eng.
1 CONT d. 1647, Long Ashton, Gloucestershire
1 CONT British proprietary founder of Maine, who promoted, though unsuccessfully, the colonization of New England along aristocratic lines.
1 CONT
1 CONT After a colourful military career in his early manhood, during which he was knighted (1591), Gorges' life after 1605 was dominated by attempts to gain royal sanction for various settlement schemes in North America, although he him
1 CONC self never traveled there. He felt that colonizing should be a royal endeavour and that colonies should be kept under rigid control from above. In 1620 Gorges succeeded in obtaining a charter to develop the Council for New England
1 CONC --a proprietary grant covering the entire area in North America between the 40th and 48th parallels. He intended to distribute the land as manors and fiefs to fellow gentry who were members of the Council but was thwarted by the s
1 CONC uccess of two vigorous, middle-class, self-governing English colonies founded by joint-stock companies at Plymouth and Massachusetts Bay. Since these New England settlements had received their charters directly from the crown, the
1 CONC  Council was thus bypassed as an intermediary.
1 CONT
1 CONT Gorges was the recipient of several land grants during his lifetime, most importantly the charter for Maine in 1639. Although his agents set up a provincial government there, the English Civil Wars and Gorges' advancing age preven
1 CONC ted him from fulfilling his American dream.
1 CONT
1 CONT A Grant of the Province of Maine to Sir Ferdinando Gorges and John Mason, esq., 10th of August, 1622
1 CONT This indenture, made the 10th day of August, Anno Dom. 1622, and in the 20th yeare of the reigne of our Sovereigne Lord James, by the grace of God King of England, Scotland, France and Ireland, Defender of the Faith, &c. Between t
1 CONC he President and councill of New-England on the one part, and Sir Ferdinando Gorges, of London, Knight, and Captain John Mason, of London, Esquire, on ye other part: Witnesseth, that whereas our said Sovereigne Lord King James, fo
1 CONC r the making of a plantation and establishing a colony or colonies in ye country called or knowne by ye names of New-England in America, hath, by his Highness Letters Patents, under the Great Seale of England, bearing date at West
1 CONC mr. the 3d day of November, in the l8th yeare of his reigne, given, granted and confirmed unto the Right Honorable Lodowick, Duke of Lenox; George, Marquis of Buckingham; James, Marquis Hamilton; Thomas, Earl of Arundell; Robert, 
1 CONC Earl of Warwick; Sir Ferdinando Gorges, Knt., and divers others whose names are expressed in ye said Letters Patents, and their successors and assignee, that they shall be one Body Politique and Corporate perpetuall, and that they
1 CONC  should have perpetual succession, &c., and one comon scale or scales, to serve for the said Body, and that they and their successors shall be known, called and incorporated by the name of the President and Councill established at
1 CONC  Plymouth in the county of Devon, for the planting, ruling and governing of New-England in America; and also hash, of his especiall grace, certaine knowledge and meer motion, for him, his heyres and successors; and given, granted 
1 CONC and confirmed unto the said President and councill, and their succesors, under the reservacons, limitacons and declaracons in the said Letters Patents expressed, all that part or porcon of that country now commonly called New-Engl
